|> Exercise
    work=ing
    handle=_relate_general_Acosx_plus_Bsinx_polar_conversion_to_cosx_plus_sinx_

    Relate Exercise >>_Acosx_+_Bsinx_as_sinusoid_ to Exercise >>_cosx_plus_sinx_as_sinusoid_.
    !! How does the result of Exercise >>_Acosx_+_Bsinx_as_sinusoid_
    !! relate to the result of Exercise >>_cosx_plus_sinx_as_sinusoid_?

    |> Solution

        To recall, Exercise >>_cosx_plus_sinx_as_sinusoid_
        establishes that

        $$
        \cos x + \sin x = \sqrt{2}\cos(x - \eta/2)
        $$

        but this is the special case 
        $$
        A = B = 1
        $$
        of
        Exercise >>_Acosx_+_Bsinx_as_sinusoid_ since

        $$
        \cos x + \sin x = 1\cdot \cos x + 1\cdot \sin x
        $$

        and since

        $$
        (\sqrt{2}, \eta/2)
        $$

        are the (“the”) polar coordinates of the point $(1, 1) \in \rr^2$!